"Create or open a C# program in Visual Studio.

Right click on your program in Solution Explorer.

Click Add Reference...

Under .NET, select System.Data.Linq

Click OK.

Add the following lines to the top of the source code:

```c#
using System.Linq;
using System.Data.Linq;
using System.Data.Linq.Mapping;
```

Note: Visual Studio may have already added the line for System.Linq.

Create a new DataContext class by entering this code, replacing login_string with your actual login string:

```c#
class MyDatabase : DataContext
{
        private const String LoginString = "login_string";

        public MyDatabase() : base(LoginString)
        {
        }
}
```

Add this line to your Main method to create a new instance of the MyDatabase class:

```c#
MyDatabase db = new MyDatabase();
```

The DataContext instance acts as your connection to SQL Server.

Add this line your Main method to create a new table in your database:

```c#
db.ExecuteCommand("Create table employees.");
```

Add the following lines to your source code:

```c#
public class Employee
{
        public int ID;
        public string Name;
        public string Department;
}
```

This will define a new class, with the Table attribute indicating that the class represents table data, the Name parameter associating a name for that table, the Column attribute indicating column names and types, and the IsPrimaryKey parameter indicating the primary key column.

Add this line to the MyDatabase class:

```c#
public Table<Employee> Employees;
```

In your Main method, create three new rows of data with new instances of the Employee class and filling in the data, as follows:

```c#
Employee employee1 = new Employee();
employee1.ID = 101;
employee1.Name = "John Smith";
employee1.Department = "Sales";
db.Employees.InsertOnSubmit(employee1);

Employee employee2 = new Employee();
employee2.ID = 102;
employee2.Name = "Ted";
db.Employees.InsertOnSubmit(employee2);
```

Note: Only the names of the employees are displayed in the output.

"Start by creating or opening a new C# program in Visual Studio. In the Solution Explorer, right-click on your project and select 'Add Reference...'. Under the '.NET' tab, choose 'System.Data.Linq'.

At the top of your code, add these using statements:

```c#
using System.Linq;
using System.Data.Linq;
using System.Data.Linq.Mapping;
```

Next, create a new DataContext class by typing the following code, replacing 'login_string' with your actual database connection information:

```c#
class MyDatabase : DataContext
{
        private const String LoginString = "login_string";

        public MyDatabase() : base(LoginString)
        {
        }
}
```

In your main method, create a new instance of the MyDatabase class:

```c#
MyDatabase db = new MyDatabase();
```

With this connection to your database, you can now create a new table using the following code:

```c#
db.ExecuteCommand("Create table employees.");
```

Then, define a new class called 'Employee' with columns 'ID', 'Name', and 'Department'.

Finally, add this line to your MyDatabase class:

```c#
public Table<Employee> Employees;
```

In your main method, create some sample data by creating new Employee objects and inserting them into the 'Employees' table:

```c#
Employee employee1 = new Employee();
employee1.ID = 101;
employee1.Name = "John Smith";
employee1.Department = "Sales";
db.Employees.InsertOnSubmit(employee1);

Employee employee2 = new Employee();
employee2.ID = 102;
employee2.Name = "Ted";
db.Employees.InsertOnSubmit(employee2);
```

Remember, this code shows only the names of the employees in the output."

## How to Create a Secure Login Script in PHP and MySQL

**Install a Web Server, PHP and MySQL on your server.**

Before you can create a secure login script, you need to install a web server like Apache or Nginx alongside PHP and MySQL on your server.


**Create a MySQL database.**

```sql
CREATE DATABASE loginapp;
```

**Create a user with only SELECT, UPDATE and INSERT privileges.**

```sql
CREATE USER 'loginuser'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'strongpassword';
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE, INSERT ON loginapp.* TO 'loginuser'@'localhost';
```

**Create a MySQL table named "members".**

```sql
CREATE TABLE members (
id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
email VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
password VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL
);
```

**Create a table to store login attempts.**

```sql
CREATE TABLE login_attempts (
id INT PRIMARY KEY AUTO_INCREMENT,
username VARCHAR(50) NOT NULL,
attempt_time DATETIME NOT NULL
);
```

**Create a test row in the table "members".**

```sql
